      "raw_response": "REFUTED\n\nThe abstract suggests that higher intake of folate and vitamin B6 is associated with a reduced risk of coronary heart disease (CHD), which contradicts the claim that these vitamins increase levels of homocysteine. Homocysteine is an amino acid that, when elevated, is considered a risk factor for CHD. The study's findings suggest that adequate intake of folate and vitamin B6, above the recommended dietary allowance, may actually help lower homocysteine levels and reduce the risk of CHD.",
